The as-sintered specimens were precisely machined into cylindrical compression samples (ϕ3 mm × 4.5 mm) via wire electrical discharge machining. Uniaxial compression tests were conducted on an INSTRON-5982 universal testing machine equipped with a mechanical extensometer at a strain rate of 10 –3  s −1 . Vickers microhardness was measured using an FM-ARS 9000 microhardness tester under a load of 100 gf with a dwell time of 15 s. A minimum of three replicate tests were performed for each condition to ensure data reliability. Tribological properties were evaluated using a ball-on-disk reciprocating friction and wear tester (MFT-R4000, Lanzhou Institute of Chemical Physics). Tests were carried out at ambient temperature in air, with Si 3 N 4  ceramic balls (diameter 5 mm, hardness ~ 1500 HV) serving as the friction pair. The reciprocating stroke was 5 mm, the sliding velocity 0.02 m/s, the normal load 10 N, and the total sliding duration 1,800 s. Each test was repeated at least three times to ensure reproducibility. Wear track morphologies were characterized using a Keyence VK-X1100 laser scanning confocal microscope for three-dimensional surface mapping. The wear volume loss (ΔV) and specific wear rate (mm 3 /Nm) were calculated based on the wear scar depth ( h ), width ( b ), and length ( L ). The wear volume was determined according to Eq. ( 1 ): 3 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
